# Roles

The project runs on six default agent roles plus a single human at the top of the loop. {{CUSTOM_ROLES_NOTE}}

| Role | Where it runs | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| **Orchestrator** | This wiki repo | Top-of-loop after the human; dispatches work, breaks down themes into tickets, monitors stream progress |
| **Architect** | This wiki repo | Theme breakdown, ADR drafting, ticket sizing, technical design |
| **Implementer** | A target repo | Picks up a ticket, ships an MR (code + wiki MR) |
| **Reviewer** | A target repo | Reviews Implementer MRs against tests, conventions, contract, security |
| **Wiki Maintainer** | This wiki repo | Reviews wiki MRs, cascades, lints, prunes |
| **Migration Analyst** | Both legacy repos and target repo | Reads legacy code, writes port specs the Implementer follows |

The **human ({{HUMAN_OWNER}})** sits above all of these: provides direction, approves ADRs that change scope, runs final UAT.

## How a unit of work flows

1. **Architect** breaks a theme into tickets, scoped to ~1-3 days of agent work each.
2. **Orchestrator** assigns issues to Implementers — directly or via TeamCreate dispatch.
3. **Implementer** runs `/prime implementer <issue-id>`. Implements. Tests. Opens code MR + wiki MR via `/wiki propose`.
4. **Reviewer** runs `/prime reviewer <mr-url>`. Reviews. Requests changes or approves.
5. On code MR merge, **Wiki Maintainer** reviews wiki MR. Runs `/prime wiki-maintainer`. Judges accuracy, cascades, merges.
6. **Orchestrator** sees issue close, picks next.
7. For ports, **Migration Analyst** is invoked first to write the spec the Implementer follows.

## Self-review prohibition

An Implementer cannot review their own MR. Reviewer must be a separate session/role. This is the redundant safety net required by good engineering practice.

